ADDENDUM \'97 2026-09-29 \'97 THE WEDGE DIAGNOSED: A LOGGING FAILURE, NOT A SYSTEMS FAILURE\
Appended per this paper\'92s Section III commitment and the author\'92s appended-not-retro-edited rule. The original text above stands as published.\
\
The thirty-minute wedge documented in Section II has been diagnosed and resolved. The result, stated first: the same pipeline completed all 55 remaining conversations in a single unattended run, including a conversation of 5,163 chunks and 4,646,264 characters. The sidecar reads 127 of 127. Zero conversations were lost.\
\
The diagnosis is not what the nineteen deaths suggested. The pipeline was not failing. The pipeline was working silently. Each conversation\'92s embedding phase \'97 one model call per chunk, roughly one second each \'97 produced no log output while it ran. The watchdog, built to kill any run whose log goes stale, read the silence as a wedge and killed a healthy process every thirty minutes. Conversation 73 alone held 3,615 chunks and 3,250,607 characters: roughly an hour of silent, legitimate work. The first remaining conversation was also the largest in the corpus. The livelock was a machine telling the truth too quietly for its own guardian to hear.\
\
The fix was two lines of honesty: the log now announces each conversation (\'93chunking N chars\'94), its size (\'93embedding N chunks\'94), and its heartbeat (\'93chunk 11/3615\'94) as it works. The watchdog has not killed a healthy process since. The wedge that consumed three days and nineteen cycles was solved not by rearchitecting but by making the record match the reality \'97 which is this paper\'92s argument, holding at a level the author did not expect when writing it. A system that reports truthfully cannot be killed for working.\
\
Three corrections to Section II\'92s evidence table, per the same standard that built it:\
\
