To write your paper accurately, it is crucial to note that Alan Glynn's original 2001 novel, [The Dark Fields](https://www.google.com/search?q=the+dark+fields&kgmid=/m/03qqqbn) (later retitled [Limitless](https://www.google.com/search?q=limitless&kgmid=/m/03qqqbn)), features a significantly darker plot, a completely different ending, and different terminology than the 2011 film adaptation. [1, 2, 3] \
Here are the precise citations, text differences, and thematic contrasts between the book and the movie script based on your specific prompts:\
## 1. Eddie on the Supply/Pills: Hoarding & Running Out\
\
* \
* The Nootropic Identity: In the novel, the drug is called MDT-48, not NZT-48.\
* The Freezer Plot Point: Stashing the pills inside a freezer is a movie invention from Leslie Dixon\'92s screenplay. In the novel, Eddie Spinola does not hide his stash in a refrigerator appliance; he carries them, conceals them in his apartment, and systematically counts his rapidly depleting stash as severe side effects like "trip-switching" (displaced time perception) begin to erode his sanity. [2, 4, 5, 6] \
* \
\
## 2. Vernon's Death & The Broken Supply Line\
\
* \
* The Broken Supply Line: In Chapter 5, Eddie runs into his ex-brother-in-law, Vernon Gant, who gives him a single tablet of MDT-48.\
* The Discovery: Unlike the film, where Vernon sends Eddie out to run errands and gets shot while Eddie is away, the novel features a much more abrupt break. When Eddie returns to Vernon's Yorkville apartment building to ask for more pills, he finds the door unlocked and stumbles directly onto Vernon's murdered body. Eddie relies on his heightened MDT-48 intellect to immediately ransack the crime scene before the police arrive, uncovering Vernon's secret ledger and the master supply of pills hidden in a hollowed-out book. [4, 7, 8] \
* \
\
## 3. The Acquisition Ending (The Lab/Company)\
\
* \
* The Film Ending vs. The Book Ending: The film\'92s triumphant climax\'97where Eddie outmaneuvers Carl Van Loon by secretly buying the pharmaceutical lab and engineering the side effects out of the drug\'97does not happen in the book. [1, 3] \
* The Real Fate of Eddie Spinola: In Alan Glynn's novel, Eddie never acquires the source or a laboratory. The story concludes with a bleak, cautionary ending. Eddie becomes an unwitting casualty of a faceless pharmaceutical corporation that uses him as an illegal trial subject. The novel closes with Eddie completely out of pills, physically deteriorating, and hiding out alone in a Vermont motel room writing his final memoirs before his system inevitably shuts down. [3, 9, 10, 11] \
* \
\
## 4. The Dependency Line: "Moving Forward" or Exploding\
\
* \
* The Exact Book Wording: The specific quote regarding the manic, exhausting need to maintain momentum under the influence of the drug appears on pages 74\'9677 of the novel:\
\
"...unless I was moving forward, moving up, unless I was transmuting, transmogrifying, morphing into something else, I was probably going to, I don't know, explode\'85"\
\
[12, 13] \
* The Reality of Withdrawal: While the movie relies on Eddie's ex-wife Melissa to state that running out means corporate organ failure and death, the novel tracks this dependency through Eddie's internal monologue as a slow, agonizing neural decay. The book explicitly addresses that stopping MDT-48 results in total cognitive and physical collapse, culminating in the fatal withdrawal Eddie faces in the final pages. [1, 3, 9] \
