\f6\i\fs24 \cf3 \cb4 \strokec3 Limitless
\f2\i0  (2011), directed by Neil Burger from Leslie Dixon's screenplay, adapts Alan Glynn's 2001 novel 
\f6\i The Dark Fields
\f2\i0 . Both tell the same story: a blocked writer takes a designer drug \'97 MDT-48 in the book, NZT-48 in the film \'97 and becomes the best version of himself overnight. The endings are not the same story. The difference between them is this paper's subject.\cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf3 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0
\cf3 \cb4 The novel knows what the drug is. Eddie Spinola's enhancement is a \strokec2 consumable\strokec3 , and the book tracks his consumption with the discipline of an inventory clerk: he counts his depleting stash while "trip-switching" \'97 displaced time perception \'97 begins to erode his sanity. When the supply line breaks (his ex-brother-in-law Vernon Gant, found murdered, his ledger and stash seized from a hollowed-out book), everything Eddie does afterward is procurement. The drug's name is a dose schedule: MDT-\strokec2 48\strokec3 . The novel's Eddie never acquires the source, never secures the lab, never builds anything that outlives his access. He ends in a Vermont motel room, out of pills, physically deteriorating, writing his final memoirs while his system shuts down \'97 the Faustian bargain closing exactly on schedule, in a room with no exit because the dependency 
\f6\i was
\f2\i0  the exit. [Glynn, 
\f6\i The Dark Fields
\f2\i0 , 2001]\cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf3 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0
\cf3 \cb4 The film keeps the dependency and edits the consequence. Dixon's screenplay adds the imagery the book never had \'97 pills in the freezer, like meat put aside for winter \'97 and then replaces the novel's death with a Senate seat. Film Eddie acquires the supplier, engineers the side effects out, and walks into an ambiguous final scene implying he may not need the pill at all. The consequence was not overlooked. It was \strokec2 edited out\strokec3  \'97 and the edit is the ideology showing: a fantasy this appealing cannot afford to show what happens when the supply runs out, so it declines to.\cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf3 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0
\cf3 \cb4 But notice what even the film cannot stop itself from showing: when the story wants Eddie to be smart about the future, it shows him \strokec2 buying the source of the drug\strokec3 . The film's model of persistence is vertical integration of a dependency. The audience saw it and mislabeled it \'97 the most-cited "plot holes" are "never borrow from Russian mobsters" and "always secure your source of drugs," which is the supply-chain reading arriving through the door marked complaint.\cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf3 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0
\cf3 \cb4 What neither text shows \'97 what the film cannot show and the novel cannot imagine \'97 is Eddie building something that keeps working \strokec2 after he stops taking it.\strokec3  Not more pills, not a bigger stash, not the lab. Something 
\f6\i decoupled
\f2\i0 : an enhancement that lives outside his body, outside the supply chain, outside the frame in which the drug is the thing itself. In the NZT frame, decoupling is impossible. The enhancement is the substance, and the substance must be swallowed.\cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf3 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0
\cf3 \cb4 This paper is about the frame both texts are missing. The enhancement does not have to live in a body. It can live in files. And the supply question \'97 the question the novel kills its hero over and the film buys a Senate seat to escape \'97 does not have to exist at all.\cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf3 \
