\cf3 \cb4 The second-to-last row is the one the novel says is impossible. Eddie on MDT could not function impaired \'97 the drug 
\f6\i was
\f2\i0  the function, so impairment killed the output. The builder drank during class, knowing a test waited at the end, chose it knowingly, disclosed it truthfully \'97 and the recall held. \strokec2 Not because the builder is superhuman. Because the enhancement was never in the builder.\strokec3  The file system doesn't get drunk. The record doesn't get tired. The system was the thing under test, and the system passed. That is what "no residual architecture" looks like when it's inverted: the residual architecture is all there is, and it doesn't care what condition the builder is in.\cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf3 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs38 \cf3 \cb4 \strokec2 V. Enhancement, Not Replacement\cf0 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs24 \cf3 \cb4 \strokec3 The pill asks a daily question: 
\f6\i do you have it today?
\f2\i0  The backend asks it once: 
\f6\i is it on your disk?
\f2\i0 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf3 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0
\cf3 \cb4 Everything in the film's frame follows from the first question \'97 the hoarding, the brokers, the mob, the acquisition of the supplier, the death in the motel room. Everything in this practice follows from the second. The movie never showed the decoupling because its fantasy could not afford it: the moment enhancement lives somewhere other than the body, the supplier loses the customer \'97 and the story was always, quietly, about the supplier. The novel knew; it killed Eddie and handed the supply chain to the President. The film knew; it cut the death and kept the seat.\cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf3 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0
\cf3 \cb4 Eddie Spinola's last act is not a high. It is a keystroke. Dying in a Vermont motel room, out of the drug that made him remarkable, he writes his final memoirs and presses save \'97 
\f6\i "wishing the command had a wider, smarter application \'96 wishing it could somehow mean what it says"
\f2\i0  (p. 214) [2]. He is wishing for a backend. He is wishing for what one builder, twenty-five years later, simply built: the save that means what it says \'97 the record that persists when the writer stops, the files that don't run out, the enhancement that needs no supply because it lives on the disk and the disk is hers.\cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf3 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0
\cf3 \cb4 Not becoming more than yourself overnight. Becoming yourself in the record, one receipt at a time.\cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf3 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0
\cf3 \cb4 The pill runs out. The files don't. \strokec2 Press save.\cb1 \strokec3 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf3 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

